## 3. Growing degree

Notation: Λ_α := (log N)^α(log log N)^{1−α}, so L_N[α, c] = exp(cΛ_α). D := log N/log log N.

### 3.1 Recursion at L[α]: the shape-test barrier disappears [proved, conditional on the outer algorithm]

Suppose some design has its non-shape work bounded by L_N[α, p + o(1)] and makes L_N[α, δ + o(1)] expected shape
tests on inputs ≤ √(2N), with all the structure of §1.2. Then Lemma F holds with Λ_α in place of Λ.
- The scaling step becomes L_{√(2N)}[α, b] ≤ e·L_N[α, 2^{−α}b].
- The conclusion is c = max(p, δ/(1 − 2^{−α})).

So the cost of complete factorisation of the halves never forces α. This is lemmaM's barrier (c): HECM at
B1 = L[2/3] costs L[4/9]. The recursive replacement costs L_{√N}[α] = L_N[α, 2^{−α}c].

Every intermediate smoothness test, on a value x = exp((log N)^{γ}) with γ < 1, costs
L_x[α, c] = exp(c(log N)^{γα}…) = L_N[α]^{o(1)}. So multi-level designs need neither HECM nor batch smoothness at the
large intermediate bounds.

**Caveat.** This replaces every cost barrier of testing. It does nothing for relation supply or descent geometry.

### 3.2 Lemma S″: split counting without the 2^ω loss [proved]
## Verdict

**Fixed degree: the 1/√2 floor is gone.**
- Lenstra–Pomerance was the shape test, factoring the halves u₀, v₀ of size ≈ √N. Replacing it by a recursive call of
  the algorithm itself is rigorous, given Theorem A's proof.
- **Theorem RA [proved from Theorem A].** For each fixed (d, k, μ, η₀) admissible for Theorem A (θ < 1), and each
  fixed a > 0, the self-recursive algorithm completely factors every N. Its expected time is L_N[1/2, c(a) + o(1)],
  with c(a) = max(a + θ/(2a), (2 + √2)a).
  - The minimum is at a* = √((√2 − 1)θ/2).
  - The minimum value is **c\* = √((1 + √2)θ)**.
- Constants. These are **infima** over fixed (k, η₀), exact in `src/recurse/constants.py`. No single parameter set
  attains them. For example, at d = 9 the smallest admissible k = 13 gives c\* = 0.9952 (+η₀-slack), k = 50 gives
  0.8748 and k = 1000 gives 0.8522. The "0.8510 at d = 9" is the infimum.
  | d | 7 | 9 | 11 | 13 | 19 | 29 | 49 | 99 |
  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
  | c\* | 0.9515 | **0.8510** | 0.7769 | 0.7193 | 0.6018 | 0.4914 | 0.3806 | 0.2691 |
  | Theorem A | 0.9723 | 0.9192 | 0.8839 | 0.8586 | 0.8132 | 0.7778 | 0.7495 | 0.7283 |
  - As d → ∞, c\* → 0.
  - The threshold for c < 1 is unchanged: c\* < 1 ⟺ θ < √2 − 1 ⟺ (1 + θ)/√2 < 1. So the minimal k is the same as
    before (k ≥ 13 at d = 9, where c\* = 0.99521 instead of 0.99720).
- **Corollary P (portfolio) [proved from Theorem A; the argument is GPT-6.1-sol's r19, checked here].** One Las Vegas
  algorithm factors every N in expected time exp(o((log N log log N)^{1/2})) = L_N[1/2, o(1)]. No effective rate
  is supplied.
  - The same device makes Theorem A's 0.9192 at d = 9 a genuine "+o(1)" (thmA audit caveat 1).
- **What binds.** The shape test was the only place 1/√2 entered.
  - With recursion, the recursive shape test and the special-q search T_SQ bind together.
  - Linear algebra is slack: 2a\*/c\* = 2 − √2 ≈ 0.586.
  - The split probability costs L^{o(1)} and does not bind.
- **(T)** survives. The recursive calls see only u₀ and v₀, which are functions of R, and they use fresh coins
  (Lemma T_rec). The toy run confirms the stronger exact statement, oracle substitution (Lemma OS).
  - In 22/22 instances the outer transcript was bit-identical under the recursive oracle and under Pollard–Brent.
  - All pre-registered predictions held (§2). The DD rate was 0.484 with z = −2.2, inside the frozen |z| ≤ 3.
- **Reviews.** Two GPT-6.1-sol rounds (blind and adversarial) and two independent Opus critics (proofs; literature)
  found no fatal or major error in RA, P or S″. Every fix is applied (§5).

**Growing degree: no rigorous L[α] with α < 1/2 is obtained.**
- Recursion does remove lemmaM's barrier (c), and every other cost barrier from smoothness or shape testing, at every
  level. A shape test on √N-size halves costs T(√N) = L_N[α, 2^{−α}c]. Every intermediate smoothness test costs
  L_N[α]^{o(1)} (§3.1).
- What binds instead is the descent geometry and the precision of the relation-supply estimates.
  - **Size model, one-level descent [heuristic arithmetic; re-derived independently, agreeing with lemmaM (b) and
    r19 item 4].**
    - If the descendability density satisfies γ ≥ (log N)^{−O(1)} uniformly in d and k, the floor is α ≥ 2/5.
    - If instead log(1/γ) ≍ d^τ log log N, the floor is α ≥ (2 + τ)/(5 + 2τ). For τ = 1, which covers losses
      (log N)^{O(d)} or d^{O(d)}, this gives **3/7**.
  - **Multi-level degree-1 descent in one field.**
    - With a bounded number of levels, the size model reaches every α > 1/3, using ⌈(1 − 2α)/(3α − 1)⌉ levels.
    - With O(log log N) levels whose constants contract geometrically, it reaches **α = 1/3 itself**. My first draft
      missed this by counting exponents only; the GPT-6.1-sol blind round recurse_1 §2.5 found it, and I re-checked
      it (§3.4).
    - This needs a *nested* descendability statement: values whose prime factors lie in a field-dependent good set.
# specialfam: deterministic factoring of explicit special families

Agent `specialfam`, phase 7, 2026-10-19. Pre-registration `notes/specialfam_prereg.md` (commit
`4be7188`, made before any run). Code `src/specialfam/`, tests `tests/test_specialfam.py`, raw data
`experiments/raw/specialfam_e*.jsonl`, summary `experiments/analysis/specialfam_summary.json`.
Starting point: codex `r18_ideas.out` §1–§2.

## 0. Verdict (plain language)

- **Yes, there are rigorous, checked, deterministic algorithms below N^{1/6} for explicit infinite
  families of N, and we prove them.** But below-1/6 special families are *cheap to come by*. A prime
  in a short interval or in a residue class with a large modulus already gives a factoring exponent
  near 0 by known Coppersmith methods (§6). So the value of this note lies in the general mechanism and
  in the careful accounting, not in "beating 1/6". The families where the new mechanism is the right
  tool are those not localised in size or residue: Harman–Lewis sectors (0.15475) and Xiannan Li's
  sparse x³ + 2y³ (0.1604).
- **Main statement (Theorem G, proved; no prior formulation found by us).** Pollard–Strassen extends
  from intervals to polynomial values.
  - *Setting.* Φ is any integer polynomial of bounded degree, and the box has a side of length at
    least √(volume) along which Φ has a unit leading coefficient. All values on the (slightly
    extended) box must be nonzero and below N in absolute value.
  - *Conclusion.* If some prime of N divides some value of Φ on the box, a proper divisor of N is
    output deterministically in Õ(√volume).
  - *Engine.* The identity ∏_{s∈S} χ(s) = ±Nm(F_S mod χ) = ±Res(χ, F_S), plus one remainder tree of
    F_S modulo many small monic χ over (Z/N)[Y]. No ring with a norm is needed.
  - *Relation to r18.* r18's (Z/N)[i] construction is the special case Φ = a² + b².
  - *Boxes without a long side.* A norm form over an order (Theorem G2) restores √volume, but only for
    decomposable Φ.
- **Families (proved, via verified analytic inputs).**
  - p = a² + b² with b ≤ p^{0.119+o(1)} (Harman–Lewis): N^{0.15475+o(1)} balanced, N^{0.1238+o(1)} at
    p ≍ N^{2/5}. This confirms r18 §1.
  - Gaussian primes in small polar boxes:
    - Harman–Kumchev–Lewis 0.53, read by us: N^{0.1325+o(1)} balanced, N^{0.106+o(1)} at N^{2/5}.
    - Lewis's thesis 0.528, cited via secondary sources: 0.132 / 0.1056.
    - A 2025 preprint claim of 0.5248: 0.1312 / 0.10496, UNSURE.
  - A non-norm polynomial family below 1/6, not localised in size or residue: Xiannan Li's sparse
    x³ + 2y³ (y ≍ x^{1−γ}, γ < 5/67; refereed; Theorem 1 read by us). By Theorem G it gives
    N^{43/268+ε} = N^{0.1604+ε} balanced and N^{0.1284+ε} at N^{2/5}.
  - Coppersmith known-residue families via Linnik constants (Theorem S). These are uniform bounds over
    the least-prime family; balance is not proved.
    - 3/20 with Xylouris's published L = 5, superseding r18's 2/13 (L = 5.2).
    - 1/24 for primorial or 2-power moduli (Chang, Cor. 11).
    - 0.0132 for 3-power moduli (Banks–Shparlinski).

    These families are "low bits known up to sign" in disguise.
  - In the Theorem A/G/G2 families (HL, HKL, polynomial forms), q can be any prime of the right size.
    In the Theorem S families, q is constrained too (q² ≡ 1 mod g).
- **One improvement over a 2026 result (Corollary W, proved; small; the γ < 1/12 part is conditional
  on GFHP Thm 5.3, an unrefereed 2025 preprint).** Let g = gcd(p−1, q−1) = N^γ for
  balanced N = pq. Then N factors deterministically from N alone in N^{E**(γ)+o(1)}, where
  - E** = 1/5 − 3γ/5 on [0, 1/18],
  - E** = 1/6 on [1/18, 1/12],
  - E** = 1/4 − γ on [1/12, 1/6],
  - E** = γ/2 on [1/6, 1/4],
  - and E** equals Ward–Maestro's curve beyond 1/4.

  This is strictly below Ward–Maestro's Prop. 28.2 curve E(γ) on (0, 1/5) \ {1/12}, with equality at
  1/12. It is also strictly below their record min(1/5, E) there. At γ = 1/6 it gives 1/12
  against their 1/6. It is a composition of WM's own recovery of g (Pollard–Strassen on N − 1) with
  published deterministic decoders: Coppersmith subdivision (GFHP 2025 Cor. 3.2, Oznovich–Volk 2025
  Thm 3.2, WM Cor. 14.3) for γ ≥ 1/12, and Gao–Feng–Hu–Pan's Theorem 5.3 for γ ≤ 1/12. The
  composition was not found stated by us.
- **What does not package (in our two models).** Non-decomposable Φ on boxes whose sides are all
  ≪ √volume, e.g. x² + y² + z² on a cube. This is a model statement, not a lower bound.
- **Prior art.**
  - *Theorem G.* No prior formulation for degree ≥ 2 moduli or polynomial boxes was found by us. Every
    piece is known:
    - remainder trees of a product modulo monic f_i (Bernstein, *Fast multiplication and its
      applications*, §18–19);
    - resultant = norm;
    - degree-1 moduli with arbitrary baby/giant sets (Hittmeir 2021 Def. 3.1 "revealing" pairs;
      Harvey 2021 Alg. 4.1);
    - quadratic-extension products in P±1 stage 2 (Montgomery–Kruppa 2008) as a close relative.
  - *Theorem S.* Its decoding step is published. GFHP 2025 Thm 3.1 and Oznovich–Volk 2025 Thm 3.2
    give deterministic known-residue Coppersmith subdivision. O–V's main algorithm has the same
    "learn p mod M, then decode" shape. What remains is only the discovery of the modulus and its
    mixed signs from N² − 1, which we did not find stated.
  - *Corollary W.* Not found, including in Ward–Maestro and GFHP. GFHP's Remark 5.5 compares the two
    decoders for a *known* modulus.
- **Experiments.** 576 instances (40–100 bits) were run as pre-registered, and all seven predictions
  held.
  - Every one of the 516 promised instances was factored; the controls gave 0 wrong factors.
  - Norm-box cost slopes match the predictions: 0.1543 vs 0.15475 (balanced) and 0.1244 vs 0.1238
    (β = 2/5). The wall-time slope is 0.184, against Pollard–Strassen's 0.25.
  - Corollary W's measured slopes at γ = 1/8 and 1/6 (0.161, 0.096) already sit below WM's E(γ)
    (0.1875, 0.1667). Details in §9.
- **Reviews (all done; every valid fix applied, §10).**
  - Opus critic: ACCEPT WITH FIXES.
  - Opus prior-art auditor.
  - GPT-6.1-sol adversarial referee (`codex/specialfam_1.out`): MAJOR REVISION, no fatal obstruction,
    Corollary W's improvement confirmed.
  - GPT-6.1-sol literature audit (`codex/specialfam_2.out`): found stronger family inputs, now in the
    table.

## 1. Setting and cost model

- N ≥ 2 is given. "Deterministic" means no randomness; correctness is unconditional.
- Every output is a verified proper divisor, so false outputs are impossible.
- Bit operations are counted with M(n) = O(n log n) for n-bit integer multiplication (Harvey–van der
  Hoeven 2021). Arithmetic in Z/N costs O(M(log N)).
- A product of two degree-< s polynomials over Z/N costs O(M(s(log N + log s))) by Kronecker
  substitution. For s ≤ N this is O(s log² N).
- Division with remainder of a degree-< 2s polynomial by a *monic* degree-s polynomial over any
  commutative ring A costs O(1) multiplications of size s. Use Newton iteration for the inverse of the
  reversed divisor modulo Y^s. Its constant term is 1, so no element of A is inverted (Bernstein,
  *Fast multiplication and its applications*, §6 and §17; also von zur Gathen–Gerhard, Ch. 9–10).
- Hence a product tree, and a remainder tree, over K monic polynomials of total degree D costs
  O(M(D log N) log K) bit operations. For D ≤ N this is O(D log³ N).
- AGENT_RULES: no factorization or primality routine is used by operational code. Flint is used only
  for multiplication and `%` by monic polynomials (the audited operations, `src/common/README.md`).

